From 702408519721cf9491cff5863f180476dac3457b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:01:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix union pointer to const union pointer conversion

A `union T *` could not be passed, assigned, returned or used to
initialise a `const union T *` or a `volatile union T *`: error 3012
"Cannot assign incompatible types 'const union u*' != 'union u*'"
(3018 for a return). The same conversion works for struct and scalar
pointers. Declaration::IsSubType returned false for every
DT_TYPE_UNION, and a qualified union type is a separate Declaration,
so the identity check at the top did not help. IsSameMutable and
IsConstSame had a struct-only branch as well, so pointers to a union
and to a const union could not be compared or subtracted, a union's
copy constructor was not recognised, and in C++ an overload taking a
pointer to const union was not viable for a pointer to the union at
all, so f(const void*) was called instead; fixing IsSubType alone
would make it viable but tie it with f(const void*) at the same rank.

Unions now take the struct branch of all three predicates. Dropping a
qualifier or mixing two differently tagged unions remains an error.

unionptrconsttest.c covers the argument, assignment, initialisation
and return forms with const, volatile and (through a typedef) const
volatile, a typedef'd union, a pointer that already has one qualifier
gaining the other, comparison and subtraction, an array of pointers to
const union and a struct control. unionptroverloadtest.cpp covers
overload resolution against const void*, an ellipsis and int,
reference binding, comparison and the copy constructor.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01J5rsu7nnbQYnRY7GP34gdn
